{
  "dom_id": "step-q266076",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/6427becc41fd736c4161fd41fce92e76352315fd535132c1c8804b5d0949587e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta $ measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.26,$ calculate the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ to the nearest integer.\n\n${\\theta }_{R}≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$",
  "worked_solution": "The reference angle is the acute angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/403edda1121bb4a647a52fcff755659ab1f9fe2ea310572b13be423cef4d6db9.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/38a60a712b18a4b9575ed812f8bd184a56ae5969fe865b9edc12f2b0faccd48f.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.26}{1}=0.26.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.26)≈{75}^{∘}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
